<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"url":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/","@type":"LocalBusiness","email":"contact@geotechnical-engineering.biz","address":{"addressLocality":"Dundee","addressCountry":"UK","@type":"PostalAddress"},"areaServed":{"name":"Dundee","@type":"City"},"@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/#org-c014","name":"Geotechnical Engineering Dundee","geo":{"longitude":0.0,"@type":"GeoCoordinates","latitude":0.0}},{"description":"Prefabricated vertical drain design for Dundee's deep soft clay and peat deposits. BS EN 1997 compliant solutions to accelerate consolidation and control settlement.","url":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/","provider":{"@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/#org-c014"},"@type":"Service","areaServed":{"name":"Dundee","@type":"City"},"serviceType":"Prefabricated vertical drain (PVD) design","@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/#service-c014","name":"Prefabricated Vertical Drain Design in Dundee – Soft Ground Solutions"},{"url":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/","@type":"WebPage","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/#website-c014"},"about":{"@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/#service-c014"},"inLanguage":"en","@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/","name":"Prefabricated Vertical Drain Design in Dundee – Soft Ground Solutions"},{"url":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/","@type":"WebSite","inLanguage":"en","@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/#website-c014","name":"Dundee Uk"},{"@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://dundee-uk.geotechnical-engineering.biz/prefabricated-vertical-drain/#faq-380f","mainEntity":[{"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The soft alluvial and estuarine clays along the Tay estuary, particularly where natural drainage paths are blocked by silt laminations or peat layers. Sites near the waterfront, such as Riverside or the Port area, typically show consolidation times reduced by 60–80% with properly spaced PVDs."},"name":"What soil conditions in Dundee most benefit from prefabricated vertical drains?","@type":"Question"},{"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A water table at 1.5 to 3 m depth means the mandrel must displace saturated soft ground, which can cause temporary heave and remoulding. We account for this by increasing the smear zone diameter in the design and specifying a slower penetration rate (below 0.3 m/s) to minimise soil disturbance."},"name":"How does the shallow water table in Dundee affect PVD installation?","@type":"Question"},{"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For embankment and tank farm projects, we commonly specify triangular grids with spacing between 1.2 m and 2.5 m. Tighter spacing (1.2–1.8 m) is used where the clay is thicker (>12 m) or the available surcharge period is limited to less than 6 months."},"name":"What is the typical range for band drain spacing in Dundee projects?","@type":"Question"},{"acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We install vibrating wire piezometers at mid-depth between drains and settlement plates at the ground surface. Pore pressure readings are plotted against Asaoka's method to confirm that the coefficient of consolidation matches the design value. If dissipation is slower than expected, we recommend additional drains or extended surcharge time."},"name":"How do you verify that the PVD design is working during construction?","@type":"Question"}]}]}</script> <style>/*afx*/@media(max-width:900px){.layout{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
